# Run one kitting task, top to bottom. This is the operator's entry point.
#
# The manager takes it from here: decide the starting scene state, get a plan,
# drive the policy through the plan's subtasks, verify the boundaries the
# recognizer can verify, retry what fails, and decide when the goal has been
# reached. The caller supplies a goal name and gets a report.
#
# An action because an episode is long-running, is worth watching, and is
# worth cancelling. The result is the completion report -- the thing that
# says whether this rollout is a success to learn from.

# kit | pick | place. The goal decides the plan, and the plan decides where
# the task ends: a `pick` goal finishes at `pick`, a `kit` goal at `place`.
#
# This is the one field that makes the ending move. The value head predicts
# the end of an episode well but reads the same whichever goal sentence it is
# given (-0.383 vs -0.385 on the same frames), so the goal has to reach the
# ending through the plan, not through the prompt.
string goal

# Force the starting scene state instead of deciding it: target | flipped |
# in_gripper. Empty uses the configured mode (recognizer, or the subtask head
# when no recognizer is loaded). A held part is detected from gripper effort
# first either way.
string initial_state

# Passed through to the policy. <= 0 uses the configured value.
float32 speed_scale
float32 max_episode_s

# Record this rollout through the data collector (START on begin, SAVE on
# end, including failures -- a failed rollout is the next training batch and
# the material for advantage estimation). Default comes from the config; set
# `override_record` to use this field instead.
bool record
bool override_record
---
# True only when the goal was reached. A rollout that was stopped, timed out,
# or ran out of retries reports false and still carries a full report.
bool success

# done | stopped | aborted | needs_operator
string outcome

# Why it ended. For needs_operator this is what the operator has to resolve.
string reason

# What decided the task was finished:
#   recognizer_tray    -- tray probe said ok, twice (a kit goal)
#   last_subtask_value -- value head crossed the threshold N times in a row
#                         (a pick goal, or no recognizer loaded)
#   unverified         -- the policy finished `place` but the release was
#                         never seen, so completion timed out without a check
#   already_satisfied  -- the goal held at episode start
# Empty when the task did not complete.
string basis

# Scene state the plan was built for, and the mat verdict behind it.
string initial_state

# The main prompt the goal resolved to.
string main_prompt

float32 duration_s

# Per-subtask trace as JSON: [{name, t_start, t_end, outcome, verdict}].
# JSON rather than a message array because the verdict is a probe-dependent
# probability map, and this is a record to file with the episode, not
# something a node branches on.
string subtasks_json

# Full report as JSON, the same object published on the manager's
# task_complete topic.
string completion_json

# Retry counts per subtask, as parallel arrays.
string[] retry_subtasks
uint32[] retry_counts
---
# Live progress, ~1 Hz and on every state change.
string goal_state
string subtask
string[] remaining
bool on_last_subtask
float32 subtask_elapsed_s
float32 task_elapsed_s

# Consecutive hits of the completion condition on the last subtask. Visible
# because it is what the task is waiting on at the end.
uint32 completion_streak
uint32 total_retries

float32 value_subtask
float32 value_episode

# Last event, as the manager logged it: "advance: pick -> move",
# "verdict: place: bad", "replan: place -> approach_pick (...)".
string event
